Chapter: StrideProbe

 One boot, one photograph, one question: what is this panel's real scanline
 pitch?

 The payload is told PixelsPerScanLine by the firmware and steps rows by it.
 On the ASUS TUF that value is 2048 against a visible width of 1920, and the
 symptoms there -- glyphs stretched, scanlines skipped, later text landing on
 top of earlier text, content running off the bottom -- are what you get when
 the pitch you STEP BY is larger than the pitch the display SCANS OUT. The same
 payload renders correctly in every bed we own, where the two are equal, so no
 emulator here can tell them apart.

 So draw two vertical bars into the same framebuffer, each stepping rows by a
 different candidate pitch. Exactly one of them can be vertical.

 - CYAN bar, stepped by the firmware's PixelsPerScanLine.
 - YELLOW bar, stepped by the visible width.

 If the CYAN bar is straight, the reported stride is right and the fault is
 elsewhere. If the YELLOW bar is straight, the panel scans at its visible width
 and the reported stride is the wrong number to step by. A sheared bar walks
 diagonally across the screen; there is no ambiguous outcome, and it needs no
 font, no text and no working renderer to read.

 Drawn with gop-fill-rect, which takes the row pitch as an ordinary argument,
 so this needs no emulator feature and no new drawing code.

  cites Works chapter GopHandoff
  cites Works chapter GopDraw

Section: Probe

  sp-bg : Integer = 2105376
  sp-cyan : Integer = 65535
  sp-yellow : Integer = 16776960
  sp-white : Integer = 16777215

  opening : Nothing -> [Console] Nothing
  opening (dummy) =
    let base = boot-fb-base 0
    in let w = boot-fb-w 0
    in let h = boot-fb-h 0
    in let stride = boot-fb-stride 0

 Clear using the larger of the two candidates so the whole aperture is covered
 whichever one is real, otherwise the leftover contains the previous payload's
 pixels and reads as a rendering fault of its own.

    in let clear = gop-fill-rect base stride 0 0 w h sp-bg

 A solid band across the top, stepped by the reported stride. If the reported
 stride is wrong this band alone will already be ragged.

    in let top = gop-fill-rect base stride 0 0 w 40 sp-white

    in let bar-a = gop-fill-rect base stride 200 0 24 h sp-cyan

    in let bar-b = gop-fill-rect base w 600 0 24 h sp-yellow

    in print-line "strideprobe done"
